SUMMARY
  requested  allocated   |S_{0,1}| <      noise floor |S_{0,2} - 3584| <
  200        211         10^-208          10^-206
  400        404         10^-400          10^-399
  800        809         10^-804          10^-803
  1600       1618        10^-1613         10^-1611
  3200       3217        10^-3212         10^-3210

READING.  |S_{0,1}| tracks the allocated precision: at D allocated decimal
digits the returned value has absolute value near 10^{-D}.  It sits at the
size of the residual of the control sum S_{0,2}, whose exact value 3584 is
known.  So S_{0,1} vanishes to the working precision of each run, and no run
certifies a bound materially below its own noise floor.

PARI allocates more digits than are requested: realprecision 400 works to
404 digits.  That is why the 400-digit run returns a value below 10^{-400},
with about one decimal digit of margin, while its noise floor is 10^{-399}.
A statement at that precision therefore rests on the allocation rather than
on the requested precision.  The statement with margin is the one from the
main run at 3200 digits.
